==Old English==

===Etymology===
Germanic ''*haliþa-'' ‘man, hero’, from Indo-European ''*kel-'' ‘son, youth’.
Cognate with {{etyl|goh}} ''[[helid]]'' ({{etyl|deu}} ''[[Held]]''), {{etyl|osx}} ''[[helið]]'' ({{etyl|nld}} ''[[held]]''), {{etyl|fry}} ''[[held]]'', {{etyl|non}} ''[[halr]], [[hǫlðr]]'' ({{etyl|nor}} ''[[hold]]'').  The IE root is also the source of {{etyl|grc}} ''[[κελωρ]]'', {{etyl|txb}} ''kālyśke'' ‘boy’.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhæleθ/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
'''hæleþ''' {{m}} (''plural'' '''[[hæleþas]]''' or '''hæleþ''')

# [[man]], [[hero]], [[warrior]]

